//  UJITSO build-system:
//     This file defines the API for the UJITSO build system. The purpose of the UJITSO build
//     system is to allow clients to request builds of derived data representations.
//     Those representations can be cached in a derived data cache hierarchy and retrieved
//     from this cache transparently to the client.
//     The requested derived data can be built on the local machine or distributed to build
//     agents transparently to the client.
//     To allow reuse of such derived data the UJITSO build system performs input content
//     hashing transparently to the client as long as the client ensures that all inputs to
//     the build process are specified.
//     Derived data builds can have complex dependencies, which each can be
//     hashed/cached/distributed individually.
//
//  Build request:
//     Within the UJITSO build-system each request to build some derived data is represented
//     as a dictionary of typed key/value pairs.
//     Example {"OVTexture":null, "Path":String("C:\Data\Texture.png"),"Flags", uint32_t(0x1802)}
//     This dictionary is used to first select a processor that can build the derived data and
//     second to pass all necessary information on what and how to build the derived data
//     to the matched processor.
//     As a processor itself is state-less, this dictionary *must* contain everything that
//     defines the derived data to be built. While it must contain everything to define the build
//     it must not contain the data to process itself. This data can instead be accessed in external
//     sources through references inside the dictionary.
//
//  Processor:
//     The UJITSO build-system itself doesn't know how to produce derived data.
//     It only provides the necessary glue to support hashing/caching/distribution/orchestration
//     of the build process.
//     All the work to perform the building/processing of the derived data itself happens
//     inside stateless computation services called Processors.
//     Processors get registered with the UJITSO build-system, which can then match them
//     against build requests.
//     Processors implement a way to determine the dependencies of their own processing
//     in the gatherDependencies() method.
//     Once the dependencies have finished building, processors then build the derived
//     data inside the build() method. At this point the output of declared
//     dependencies can be used in the processing.
//
//  Derived data:
//     Processors can store the derived data through the UJITSO build-system in different ways:
//      Metadata: This is a (usually small) binary blob that can be used to store data
//                that describes the real derived data or the derived data itself if
//                it is small and doesn't have to be accessed in separate parts by the client.
//                The most important factor to decide whether to store the results in metadata
//                or as external storage are:
//                  1) Do I need to load parts of the data selectively? -> external storage
//                  2) Is the data big? -> external storage
//                  3) Does the data benefit from minimal latency? -> metadata
//                Metadata is embedded in the internal UJITSO build node that will be fetched to
//                determine if the build was already cached. Putting the data itself into the
//                metadata will thus save latency as it will be directly available without a further
//                cache round-trip when it is small (in contrast to external storage).
//                On the other hand metadata will be copied multiple times internally.
//      External storage: A list of keys into a key/value storage for derived data.
//                This can be used to split the derived data into multiple pieces which
//                can then be loaded independently by the client.
//                Examples are textures where the metadata stores the texture description and
//                each mip is stored with an external storage key.
//                External storage is a way to store separate results explicitly in a way that
//                is known to the build system. It is always possible to store references to any
//                external data source in the metadata. But these would not be known
//                to the build system and it cannot perform any validation whether these results
//                exist when returning a cached result. 
//      Fabric cache: The processor can produce a fabric cache that can then be retrieved
//                by the client containing all kinds of data. 
//
//    Examples of derived data:
//      Textures:
//           Metadata: Width, height, format, mipcount...
//           ExternalStorage: One block per mip
//
//      Geometry:
//          Metadata: AABB, SubmeshCount, LODcount, MemorySizePerLOD
//          ExternalStorage: One block per geometry LOD
//
//  Interfaces:
//
//     IFactory:
//      Simple factory interface to create an agent. A IDataStore must be
//      provided so that the UJITSO build-system can store the derived data.
//
//     IAgent:
//      This interface allows a client to request a build and manage that request. It also allows
//      access to the derived data once the request has succeeded. Note that the request itself is
//      asynchronous.
//
//     IService:
//      This interface is used by the host of processors to register and unregister them with the
//      UJITSO build-system. It is also used from within the Processors to perform actions against
//      the system. Note the use of DependencyHandle when methods are called from within the
//      gatherDependencies() method and BuildHandle when methods are called from within the build() method.
//
//     Processor:
//      This is the interface a Processor needs to implement to build derived data within the UJITSO
//      build-system
//
//     Agent:
//      An agent instance combines the interfaces of the UJITSO build system with specific instances of input and output services
//      Before requesting a build a client must obtain or create an AgentInstance.
//
// ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Control flow diagram of a request (build locally):
//
// Client --requestBuild--> IAgent
//                              --filter registered processors
//                              --match-->  Processor
//                              --gatherDependencies--> Processor
//                                                               --addDependency--> IService
//                                                               --addFileSources--> IService
//                                                               --addRequestTuple--> IService
//                              --compute input hash
//                              --fetchCachedResult--> DataStore
//                            cache hit:
//                              --validate external storage
//        <--return cached result--
//                            cache miss:
//                              --build--> Processor
//                                                    --store external data--> DataStore
//                                                    --create meta data--> IService
//        <--return built result--
// 
//
// ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Example of a simple non-batched build request and how to read the results:
// 
//  // Create the agent
//  IFactory* factory = carb::getFramework().acquireInterface<carb::ujitso::IFactory>();
//  Agent* agent = factory->createAgent(datagrid, datastore, nullptr, nullptr);
//
//  // Construct the build request
//  RequestBuilder<> request;                                   // Helper to build UJITSO build-system requests
//  request.add("USDStageProcessor");                           // Tag that will be used to select the relevent processor
//  request.add(IRegistry::Path, "C:/MyFiles/cube.usda");       // Path of the usd file. IRegistry::* are just some common key types
//  request.add("SparsePopulation", true);                      // Further options that determine the outcome of the processing
//  request.add("LoadAll", true);
//  request.add(IRegistry::Version, uint32_t(1));
//
//  // Request the build
//  RequestHandle requestHandle;
//  agent->requestBuild(*agent, request.getRequest().getRequest(),
//   [&](void* callbackContext0, void* callbackContext1, RequestHandle handle, OperationResult result)
//   {
//
//       // Handle the result of the build asynchronously here
//       ...
//   }, context, &requestHandle);
//  agent->waitRequest(requestHandle); // the request above is asynchronous, use this to wait for the request to finish
//
//  // Handle the result of the build synchronously here
//
//  // Check return value of the build and retrieve the result handle
//  // The result handle is used to access parts of the build result from a consistent build
//  ResultHandle resultHandle;
//  agent->getRequestResult(*agent, requestHandle, resultHandle); 
//
//  // Retrieve metadata produced by this build
//  MetaDataStructure1 metaData;
//  uint32_t metaDataSize = sizeof(metaData);
//  agent->getRequestMetaData(*agent, resultHandle, &metaData, &metaDataSize);
//
//  // Retrieve external storage keys that the build might have produced
//  ExternalStorage storage[16];
//  uint32_t storageCount = CARB_COUNTOF32(storage);
//  agent->getRequestExternalData(*agent, resultHandle, storage, &storageCount);
//
//  // Retrieve data for an external storage block from the datastore
//  std::vector<uint8_t> dataBlock;
//  copyDataBlock(datastore, "", storage[0], dataBlock);
//
// ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Example of a simple processor:
//
//  // Create the agent
//  IFactory* factory = carb::getFramework().acquireInterface<carb::ujitso::IFactory>();
//  Agent* agent = factory->createAgent(datagrid, datastore, nullptr, nullptr);
//
//  struct SimpleProcessor : ProcessorImpl<SimpleProcessor>
//  {
//     PROCESSOR_INFO(SimpleProcessor, 1, 0);
// 
//     OperationResult gatherDependenciesImpl(DependencyContext& context, DependencyJob& job, DependencyHandle handle)
//     {
//         // decode the request
//         const char* path = getRequestValueString(context.agent, job.requests, 0, IRegistry::Path);
//         ...
//         // and add dependencies here:
//         context.agent.service->addDependency(...);
//         context.agent.service->addFileSources(...);
//         context.agent.service->addRequestTupleInput(...);
// 
//         return OperationResult::SUCCESS;
//     }
// 
//     OperationResult buildImpl(BuildContext& context, BuildJob& job, BuildHandle handle)
//     {
//         // decode the request again
//         const char* path = getRequestValueString(context.agent, job.requests, 0, IRegistry::Path);
// 
//         // perform the build and produce metadata or external storage blobs
// 
//         MetaDataStructure1 metaData{...};
//         createMetaData<MetaDataStructure1>(context.agent, handle) = metaData;
//
//         std::vector<uint8_t> binaryBlob;
//         storeExternalStorage(context.agent, handle, binaryBlob.data(), binaryBlob.size());
// 
//         return OperationResult::SUCCESS;
//     }
//  };
//
//
//  // Register a processor in the global registry (this can be done in plugin init/shutdown)
//  carb::ujitso::IRegistry* registry = carb::getFramework()->tryAcquireInterface<carb::ujitso::IRegistry>();
//  SimpleProcessor processor;
//  KeyTokenEx token{"USDStageProcessor"};
//  registry->registerProcessor(*agent, processor, { &token, 1 });
//
//  // Now requests with the token "USDStageProcessor" will be handled by the build system through this processor.
//
//  // unregister the processor
//  registry->unregisterProcessor(*agent, processor);
//
//  // More examples: kit-sdk\rendering\source\tests\test.unit\ujitso\TestUJITSO.cpp
//
// ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Advanced topics:
// ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  Build dependencies:
//  A processor can emit dependencies to other build requests during the gatherDependencies() call.
//  To do this the processor constructs a new request dictionary and passes it to the service via. addDependency().
//  gatherDependencies() will be called on every emitted dependency and requests will be built before the
//  build() function of the dependent processor is called. But the build() function will *not* be called
//  when the dependent processor is already cached. In that case the dependencies are just used to calculate
//  the compound input hash of the dependent processor, which is then used to determine the cache key
//  to access the cached results.
//  When the dependent processor's build() function is called the processor can access the results of it's
//  dependencies through the getDependencies() function. This returns an array of BuildHandle, which
//  can be used to access the metadata, external storage keys or fabric cache. These handles can also be used
//  to access the dependencies of those builds.
//  Note that the order in the BuildHandle vector matches the order of addDependency() and the processor can
//  use this to discern the BuildHandles. E.g. a processor may asked for a material dependency first and then
//  for a geometry dependency. Such a processor could assume that the material results are the first BuildHandle
//  and the geometry the second from a call to getDependencies().
//  A processor can also use the combination of getRequestForDependency() and getRequestDescription() to access
//  the request description directly.
//
// ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  External storage validation:
//  Derived data caches are only semi-durable. This means that any key/value can be purged from the cache
//  at any time without the application being informed about this.
//  To mitigate this the application must be prepared for external storage to not be available, even if a
//  build was originally found in the cache when requestBuild() was called.
//  Various tools in the IAgent and IService help with this:
//   * When storing external data blobs the application can specify the type of automatic validation to be
//     performed by the build system when a request is considered cached.
//     - Mandatory validation will immediately verify that all external storage blobs are in the cache at this
//      point in time before considering a build cached. If any blob is is missing, the request will be built.
//     - Deferred validation will push the request on an internal validation queue and return the build as cached.
//       If the validation queue later determines that am external storage blob is missing, the request will
//       be rebuilt.
//     - Preloaded validation actual loads the data for that external storage blob into memory. This is the only
//       mode that really guarantees that the storage will be present when the application requests it.
//   * When an external storage blob belonging to a cached build is unaccessible to the application later,
//     the application can call IAgent::signalExternalStorageFailure to inform the build system that the
//     associated request needs to be rebuilt immediately.
//
// ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  Building dependencies through build requests:
//  Sometimes determining the dependencies of a build is itself a costly operation that should be cached.
//  E.g. consider finding all the header files included in the compilation of a given c++ file.
//  As gatherDependencies() of the full tree doesn't allow accessing any build result before we determine whether
//  the root of the tree was cached, the types of dependencies created via addDependency() cannot be used
//  to solve this problem. Instead a processor might create another independent build request through
//  requestBuild() and then wait for and use it's results inside gatherDependencies(). Caution is necessary
//  with this approach as it will cause that particular fiber to wait until this "inner" build is finished.
//  Automatic dirty tracking is currently also not possible with this kind of dependency as the request
//  must be destroyed before gatherDependencies() returns.
// 
// ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  Containers:
//  Containers are a way to deliver application data to a processor (local or remote) without the need to
//  materialize this data in memory when a request is already cached. This allows warm loads (when everything
//  is successfully cached) to execute fast as they do not needs to ever produce all the data that would only
//  be necessary if the cache must be rebuilt.
//  To achieve this goal, container instances must only provide a compound content hash of all it's (future)
//  contents when they are created and added to a request. Only when a request is found to not be cached
//  and prior to build() being called, the container method fill() will be called by the build system to
//  instruct the container that it's full contents will be required.
//  Containers also provide an interface to serialize their contents. This interface is only invoked in
//  case a container needs to be moved to a different process to be used as an input to a build there.
//  
// ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  Job distribution
//  The dependency gathering step of all builds currently happens on the local process.
//  Once a build was found to not be cached, it is inserted in the execution queue. This queue is then emptied based
//  on the resource availabilities influenced by each request's estimateResourceRequirements().
//  Instead of inserting a job into the execution queue of the local process it can also be sent to a remote process for
//  execution. This requires a ITaskAgent instance to be provided during creation of the UJITSO agent instance.
// 
//  Control flow diagram of a request being build remotely:
//
//  cache miss:
//      IAgent --launch task--> ITaskAgent
//                                        ...send message...> ITaskService
//                                                                        --executeTask--> ITaskExecutor
//                                                                                                      --build--> Processor
//                                                                        <--succeedTask--
//                                        <...send message...
//             <-- callback --
// 
// 
// ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  File access and tracking:
//  When a processor specifies files as an input dependency, the build system determines the correct input hash
//  that is used to compute the compound input hash for the whole request based on the URI of the file(s).
//  When a local file is specified, the build system will first try to look up the content hash of the file+modified data in
//  the derived data cache. If this content hash isn't found yet, the file is loaded and the content hash computed and
//  stored in the derived data cache.
//  For remote URIs the build system either relies on the content hash being provided by omniclient (e.g. for files stored in nucleus)
//  or it will use a remote URI + modified date directly to compute a replacement for a content hash. In the latter
//  case there will be no de-duplication between cached results for identical file inputs stored at separate locations.
//  When adding file inputs the build system can also start change tracking on those files. If any of the tracked files
//  changes, a new build of all requests with that input will be built. This propagates to dependent builds, which
//  are also rebuilt, down to a root request issued by the application.
// 

using OperationResult = omni::utils::OperationResult;

enum class ValidationType
{
    MANDATORY = 0, // Needs to be validated immediately, before creating a build
    DEFERRED = 1, // Not mandatory, can be validated later
    NO_VALIDATION = 2, // No validation is required
    PRELOADED = 3, // the data is loaded at the same time as the metadata and the build immediately fails if the preloading fails
};

// Where a build is cached
enum class CacheBehaviorType
{
    Default = 0,                    // Build is cached at every level
    NoCaching = 1,                       // Build is not cached
    Local = 2,                      // Build is cached only locally
    LocalAndCluster = 3,            // Build is cached locally and in cluster
    LocalAndClusterAndRemote = 4,    // Build is cached locally, in cluster, and remotely
    ForceBuild = 5,                 // Build is not cached and build every time it is requested
};

// How the cache datastore key is calculated
enum class CacheKeyHashType
{
    Content,    // Based on cache contents
    Input       // Based on build request
};

// Determines how the processor::build() is executed
enum class BuildExecutionType
{
    Fiber,    // Processor is executed as a carb fiber (default)
    Thread     // Processor is executed as a dedicated thread
};

// NOTE: only affects jobs launched in carb fibers
// Determines the priority of the processor::build()
enum class BuildExecutionPriority
{
    Low,    // Processor is executed at low priority (default)
    Medium, // Processor is executed at medium priority
    High    // Processor is executed at high priority
};

// Determines the lifetime of dependent requests
enum class DependencyLifetime
{
    KeepAfterBuild, // Keep dependent requests alive after building (default)
    DiscardAfterBuild // Discard dependent requests after build
};

// Determines the release of requests once the handle has been destroyed
enum class RequestRelease
{
    Deferred, // Release of the associated resources is deferred
    Immediate // Resources associated with the request are immediately released during request handle destruction
};

